GUIDES = [
dict(slug="courthouse-visit-guide", cat="Court basics", issue="other", read=7,
title="Visiting the Courthouses in 33128: Security, Parking, Transit and What to Wear",
desc="A practical first-timer's guide to the downtown Miami courthouse district — security screening, Metrorail and parking, interpreters, accessibility and courtroom etiquette.",
body="""
<p>ZIP code 33128 is small — well under half a square mile — but it holds an unusual concentration of courts. Federal judges, bankruptcy trustees, family-court magistrates and probate clerks all work within a few blocks of the Government Center Metrorail station. If you have a hearing, a summons or a filing to make, this guide covers everything that happens <em>before</em> you reach the courtroom.</p>
<h2 id="which">1. Confirm which building you need</h2>
<p>The single biggest mistake first-time visitors make is going to the wrong courthouse. Federal matters (including federal jury service) are on North Miami Avenue; family and probate matters are at the Lawson E. Thomas Courthouse Center on NW 1st Avenue; deeds and official records are recorded at Courthouse East on NW 1st Street; and general civil suits are filed at the historic county courthouse on West Flagler Street just across the ZIP line. Read your notice carefully — it lists the building, floor and courtroom. Our <a href="../courthouses.html">courthouse directory</a> and the "Which building?" finder translate case types into addresses.</p>
<h2 id="security">2. Security screening</h2>
<p>Every courthouse here uses airport-style screening: walk-through magnetometers and X-ray belts for bags. Lines are longest between 8:15 and 9:15 a.m., when most calendars start. Plan on 20–45 minutes on a busy morning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Leave at home:</strong> knives of any size, scissors, pepper spray, multitools, tools and anything that could be used as a weapon.</li>
<li><strong>Bring:</strong> a government-issued photo ID and your paperwork.</li>
<li><strong>Phones:</strong> generally allowed in public areas but must be silenced; photography and recording in courtrooms are restricted unless a judge permits them. Federal courthouses have their own electronic-device rules — check the court's website before you go.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="transit">3. Getting there: Metrorail beats driving</h2>
<p>Government Center station sits underneath the Stephen P. Clark Center and connects Metrorail with the free Metromover loops. Riders heading to the federal courthouse can take the Inner Loop Metromover one stop to the Wilkie D. Ferguson, Jr. station. Most 33128 buildings are a five-minute walk from the station, and downtown Miami is one of the most walkable areas in South Florida.</p>
<p>If you must drive, budget for garage parking and give yourself extra time — street meters fill early and time limits are strict. Federal jurors should follow the parking instructions printed on their summons, which may include reimbursement.</p>
<h2 id="dress">4. What to wear and how to behave</h2>
<p>Dress as you would for a job interview: clean, conservative clothing; no hats in the courtroom; no clothing with slogans. Stand when the judge enters, address the judge as "Your Honor," speak only when it's your turn, and never interrupt. Bring a sweater — courtrooms are famously cold.</p>
<h2 id="language">5. Interpreters and accessibility</h2>
<p>Miami is one of the most multilingual court systems in the country. If you or a witness needs an interpreter, ask the clerk or the court's interpreter office <strong>well before</strong> your hearing date. Requests for disability accommodations under the ADA should also be made in advance; your summons or notice usually names the office to contact and the deadline.</p>
<h2 id="kids">6. Children</h2>
<p>Many courtrooms do not allow young children unless they are part of the case. Arrange child care in advance. Family-court visitors should check whether a supervised waiting area is available.</p>
<h2 id="clerk">7. What the clerk can and cannot do</h2>
<p>Clerks can explain procedures, accept filings, give you forms and tell you fees. By law they <strong>cannot</strong> give legal advice — they can't tell you which form to choose for your situation, what to argue or whether you'll win. For that you need an attorney or a legal-aid program. If you want an attorney to look at your situation, our <a href="../free-case-review.html">free case review</a> is the fastest route.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick checklist:</strong> photo ID · printed notice and case number · three copies of documents · charged phone on silent · arrive 45 minutes early. Build a custom one with our <a href="../tools.html#checklist">court-day checklist</a>.</div>
"""),

dict(slug="jury-duty-miami", cat="Court basics", issue="other", read=6,
title="Jury Duty in Miami: State vs. Federal Summons and What to Expect",
desc="Received a jury summons for downtown Miami? Learn the difference between state and federal jury service, how to request a postponement, what a typical day looks like and how pay works.",
body="""
<p>Two different court systems can summon you for jury duty in downtown Miami — and they run separate processes. Figuring out which one sent your summons is step one.</p>
<h2 id="which">State or federal?</h2>
<table><tr><th></th><th>State (Miami-Dade County)</th><th>Federal (Southern District of Florida)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Who sends it</td><td>Clerk of the Court for Miami-Dade County</td><td>U.S. District Court jury office</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical cases</td><td>Civil disputes, state crimes</td><td>Federal crimes, federal civil cases</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where you report</td><td>Location printed on the summons</td><td>Wilkie D. Ferguson, Jr. U.S. Courthouse, 400 N. Miami Ave (33128) for Miami</td></tr>
<tr><td>Length</td><td>Often one day or one trial</td><td>Can involve a term of on-call service</td></tr></table>
<p>The summons letterhead tells you which court sent it. Follow <em>that</em> court's instructions — online questionnaires, call-in numbers and postponement rules differ.</p>
<h2 id="postpone">Postponements and excusals</h2>
<p>Both systems allow you to request a postponement for conflicts such as travel, exams or medical appointments, and both have specific excusal categories set by law or court policy. Request it before your reporting date using the method on the summons. Ignoring a summons is not an option: courts can issue orders to show cause to people who fail to appear.</p>
<h2 id="day">What a typical day looks like</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check in</strong> at the jury assembly room after security screening.</li>
<li><strong>Orientation</strong> — usually a short video explaining the jury's role. The U.S. Courts' own overview video is in our <a href="../videos.html">video library</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting</strong> — bring something to read. You may wait hours before being called.</li>
<li><strong>Voir dire</strong> — the judge and lawyers ask a panel questions to select an impartial jury. Answer honestly; it's not a test.</li>
<li><strong>Selected or released.</strong> Many people are released the same day.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="pay">Pay and your employer</h2>
<p>Jurors receive a modest daily fee set by law, and federal jurors may be reimbursed for certain travel and parking costs. Florida law protects employees from being fired or threatened for serving on a jury; federal law gives similar protection for federal service. Ask the jury office for a certificate of attendance to give your employer.</p>
<h2 id="tips">Practical tips</h2>
<ul><li>Take Metrorail to Government Center — then the Inner Loop Metromover one stop for the federal courthouse.</li>
<li>Wear layers; jury rooms are cold.</li>
<li>Do not research the case, visit the scene or post about it online. Judges take this seriously.</li></ul>
<div class="callout">Serving on a jury is one of the few direct ways citizens shape justice. Most people who serve say the experience was better than they expected.</div>
"""),

dict(slug="small-claims-florida", cat="Money disputes", issue="civil", read=8,
title="Small Claims Court in Miami-Dade: How to Sue (or Defend) for $8,000 or Less",
desc="A plain-English walkthrough of Florida small claims for Miami-Dade residents: the $8,000 limit, where to file, serving the defendant, the pretrial conference and what to bring.",
body="""
<p>Florida does not have a separate "small claims court." Instead, the <strong>County Court</strong> handles disputes of <strong>$8,000 or less</strong> (not counting costs, interest and attorney's fees) under simplified Small Claims Rules. It's designed so ordinary people can resolve a broken contract, unpaid invoice, security-deposit fight or minor car-damage claim without a lawyer.</p>
<h2 id="fit">Is your case a good fit?</h2>
<ul><li>The amount is $8,000 or less — or you're willing to give up anything above that.</li>
<li>You can show what happened with documents: receipts, contracts, texts, photos, estimates.</li>
<li>The defendant can actually pay. Winning a judgment and collecting it are two different things.</li></ul>
<p>Above $8,000, cases up to $50,000 stay in County Court under regular civil rules; larger claims go to Circuit Court.</p>
<h2 id="where">Where to file</h2>
<p>File in the county where the defendant lives, where the problem happened, or where the property is located. In Miami-Dade, civil filings are handled by the Clerk of the Court and Comptroller; the historic courthouse at 73 W. Flagler Street (ZIP 33130) is the central civil location. Many filings can also be made through the Florida Courts E-Filing Portal.</p>
<h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Send a demand letter.</strong> Give the other side a final chance to pay with a clear deadline. Judges like to see you tried.</li>
<li><strong>Complete the Statement of Claim.</strong> Name the defendant correctly — for a business, look up its exact registered name on the Florida Division of Corporations (Sunbiz).</li>
<li><strong>Pay the filing fee</strong>, which scales with the amount claimed. Fee waivers exist for people who qualify as indigent.</li>
<li><strong>Serve the defendant</strong> through the sheriff or a certified process server. The case cannot move forward until service is completed.</li>
<li><strong>Attend the pretrial conference.</strong> This first hearing is where many cases settle, often through mediation the same day. If you don't show up, your case can be dismissed (or a default entered against you if you're the defendant).</li>
<li><strong>Trial.</strong> If there's no settlement, the judge sets a trial date. Bring three copies of every document and any witnesses.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="defend">If you've been sued</h2>
<p>Read the summons immediately and note the pretrial conference date. Show up. Bring your evidence and any counterclaim you have. If you believe you owe part of the money, mediation at the pretrial conference may be your best outcome.</p>
<h2 id="collect">Collecting a judgment</h2>
<p>If the defendant doesn't pay voluntarily, Florida provides tools such as a fact information sheet, writs of execution, bank garnishments and judgment liens. Collection can take longer than the lawsuit itself — another reason to weigh whether the defendant has assets.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Tip:</strong> Organize your evidence into a one-page timeline with exhibits labeled A, B, C. Judges hear dozens of cases a day; clarity wins.</div>
<p>Need help valuing your claim or want a lawyer's opinion? Use the <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=civil">free case review</a> form.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="traffic-ticket-miami-dade", cat="Traffic", issue="traffic", read=6,
title="Got a Traffic Ticket in Miami-Dade? Your 30-Day Options Explained",
desc="Pay, take a driving course or contest it? A clear explanation of your options for a Florida civil traffic citation, points, insurance impact and when a criminal traffic charge needs a lawyer.",
body="""
<p>A Florida civil traffic citation gives you <strong>30 days</strong> to choose how to respond. Missing that window can lead to a driver's license suspension, so act early.</p>
<h2 id="options">Your three main options</h2>
<table><tr><th>Option</th><th>What happens</th><th>Best when</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pay the fine</td><td>You're adjudicated guilty; points are added to your record</td><td>Low-point violation and clean record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elect a Basic Driver Improvement course</td><td>Pay the fine, complete an approved course; adjudication is withheld and points are not assessed</td><td>Moving violations where points would raise insurance; limited number of elections allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contest (request a hearing)</td><td>A judge or hearing officer hears the case</td><td>You have a real defense or the stakes (points, CDL) are high</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="points">Why points matter</h2>
<p>Florida suspends licenses when drivers accumulate too many points within set periods (for example, 12 points in 12 months). Even a few points can raise insurance premiums for years. The driver-course election exists precisely to keep points off your record — but you can only use it a limited number of times.</p>
<h2 id="criminal">When it's not just a ticket</h2>
<p>Some traffic offenses are <strong>criminal</strong>, not civil: driving on a suspended license, DUI, reckless driving, leaving the scene of a crash. These require a court appearance and can carry jail time and a criminal record. Talk to a criminal-defense attorney before your first court date. In Miami-Dade, many criminal traffic matters are heard at the Richard E. Gerstein Justice Building (1351 NW 12th St.).</p>
<h2 id="contest">How to contest effectively</h2>
<ul><li>Request the hearing within the 30-day window.</li>
<li>Gather evidence: photos of the intersection and signs, dash-cam footage, witness contact information.</li>
<li>Be polite and factual. If the officer doesn't appear, the case may be dismissed — but don't count on it.</li></ul>
<p>Commercial drivers and anyone close to a suspension should consider a traffic attorney; the cost is often less than years of higher premiums. <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=traffic">Ask for a free review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="florida-eviction-process", cat="Housing", issue="housing", read=8,
title="The Florida Eviction Process, Step by Step (for Tenants and Landlords)",
desc="How residential evictions work in Miami-Dade: notices, the 5-day answer deadline, depositing rent into the court registry, the writ of possession and where to find help.",
body="""
<p>Roughly 97% of homes in ZIP 33128 are renter-occupied, which makes understanding Florida's eviction process essential for residents and property owners alike. Florida's process is one of the fastest in the country — a tenant who misses a deadline can lose possession in a matter of weeks.</p>
<h2 id="notice">1. The notice</h2>
<p>Most evictions begin with a written notice. For unpaid rent, a landlord typically serves a <strong>3-day notice</strong> (excluding weekends and legal holidays) demanding payment or possession. For other lease violations, a 7-day notice is common — some allow the tenant to cure, others don't. Month-to-month tenancies are ended with a longer notice. Self-help evictions — changing locks, shutting off utilities or removing belongings — are illegal in Florida.</p>
<h2 id="complaint">2. The complaint and summons</h2>
<p>If the issue isn't resolved, the landlord files an eviction complaint in County Court. Once served, the tenant generally has <strong>5 days</strong> (excluding weekends and legal holidays) to respond to the possession claim.</p>
<h2 id="registry">3. The court registry rule — the critical step for tenants</h2>
<p>In a nonpayment case, a tenant who wants to raise any defense other than payment must generally <strong>deposit the alleged rent owed into the court registry</strong> (or file a motion asking the court to decide the amount) by the answer deadline, and keep depositing rent as it comes due. Failing to do so can result in an immediate judgment for the landlord without a hearing. This is the single most important rule tenants miss.</p>
<h2 id="judgment">4. Judgment and writ of possession</h2>
<p>If the landlord wins, the clerk issues a writ of possession. The sheriff posts it, and the tenant has <strong>24 hours</strong> before the sheriff can return to put the landlord back in possession.</p>
<h2 id="tenant-tips">Tenants: what to do now</h2>
<ul><li>Calendar the deadline the day you're served — use our <a href="../tools.html#deadline">deadline calculator</a> with "business days."</li>
<li>Contact legal aid or a tenant attorney immediately; free help may be available depending on income.</li>
<li>Keep records of every payment, repair request and photo of conditions.</li></ul>
<h2 id="landlord-tips">Landlords: avoid costly mistakes</h2>
<ul><li>Use the exact statutory notice language and count days correctly — a defective notice can force you to start over.</li>
<li>Don't accept partial rent after a notice without understanding how it affects your case.</li>
<li>Consider an attorney for contested cases; fee-shifting provisions can make it affordable.</li></ul>
<p>Get a lawyer's eyes on your notice: <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=housing">free case review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="family-court-miami", cat="Family", issue="family", read=8,
title="Family Court in Miami: Divorce, Custody and Support Basics",
desc="What to expect at the Lawson E. Thomas Courthouse Center: Florida's no-fault divorce, residency requirement, parenting plans, child support guidelines, mediation and the parenting course.",
body="""
<p>Family matters in Miami-Dade — divorce (called "dissolution of marriage" in Florida), paternity, custody, child support and domestic-violence injunctions — are centered at the <strong>Lawson E. Thomas Courthouse Center, 175 NW 1st Avenue (33128)</strong>. Domestic-violence filings are handled on the mezzanine level.</p>
<h2 id="divorce">Divorce in Florida: the essentials</h2>
<ul><li><strong>No-fault.</strong> You only need to show the marriage is "irretrievably broken" (or that a spouse has been mentally incapacitated for at least three years).</li>
<li><strong>Residency.</strong> At least one spouse must have lived in Florida for six months before filing.</li>
<li><strong>Simplified dissolution</strong> is available when both spouses agree, have no minor children, and settle property between themselves — no lawyers required in many cases.</li>
<li><strong>Equitable distribution.</strong> Marital assets and debts are divided fairly — which usually starts at equal but can be adjusted.</li></ul>
<h2 id="children">Children: time-sharing and parenting plans</h2>
<p>Florida no longer uses the terms "custody" and "visitation." Instead, parents share <strong>parental responsibility</strong> and follow a court-approved <strong>parenting plan</strong> with a time-sharing schedule. Courts decide based on the best interests of the child. Parents in a divorce with minor children are typically required to complete a parent education course before final judgment.</p>
<h2 id="support">Child support and alimony</h2>
<p>Child support follows statutory guidelines based on both parents' incomes, the number of overnights each parent has and costs like health insurance and child care. Florida's 2023 alimony reform eliminated permanent alimony and set new limits on duration; bridge-the-gap, rehabilitative and durational alimony remain.</p>
<h2 id="mediation">Mediation</h2>
<p>Most contested family cases must attend mediation before trial. A neutral mediator helps you reach agreement; anything you sign can become a binding order. Prepare by knowing your budget, your priorities and your bottom line.</p>
<h2 id="dv">Domestic violence</h2>
<p>If you're in danger, call 911. Petitions for injunctions for protection can be filed at the courthouse, and advocates are available to help. There is no filing fee for a domestic-violence injunction petition.</p>
<div class="callout">Family law is where an hour with an experienced attorney can save the most money and heartache. Start with a <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=family">free case review</a>.</div>
"""),

dict(slug="probate-florida", cat="Estates", issue="probate", read=7,
title="Probate in Miami-Dade: What Happens After Someone Dies",
desc="A clear introduction to Florida probate for families: depositing the will, summary vs. formal administration, the personal representative's role, creditors and homestead.",
body="""
<p>Probate is the court-supervised process of gathering a deceased person's assets, paying valid debts and transferring what's left to the right people. In Miami-Dade, probate filings, certified copies and wills for deposit are handled on the <strong>14th floor of the Lawson E. Thomas Courthouse Center (175 NW 1st Ave., 33128)</strong>.</p>
<h2 id="will">Step one: deposit the original will</h2>
<p>Florida law requires whoever has custody of a will to deposit it with the clerk of the county where the person lived within <strong>10 days</strong> after learning of the death. Copies aren't enough — the court needs the original.</p>
<h2 id="types">Summary vs. formal administration</h2>
<table><tr><th>Summary administration</th><th>Formal administration</th></tr>
<tr><td>For smaller estates — non-exempt assets of $75,000 or less, or when the person died more than two years ago</td><td>The standard process for larger or more complex estates</td></tr>
<tr><td>No personal representative appointed; faster and cheaper</td><td>Court appoints a personal representative (executor) who manages the estate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Often finished in weeks to a few months</td><td>Commonly 6–12 months or longer</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="attorney">Do you need a lawyer?</h2>
<p>In Florida, a personal representative in formal administration generally must be represented by an attorney (with narrow exceptions). Attorney fees are often set by agreement or by a statutory schedule based on estate value.</p>
<h2 id="creditors">Creditors and the notice period</h2>
<p>The personal representative publishes a notice to creditors and serves known creditors. Claims filed after the deadline are generally barred — one of the main protections probate provides to heirs.</p>
<h2 id="homestead">Florida homestead</h2>
<p>Florida's homestead rules are unique: a primary residence may be protected from most creditors and may have restrictions on who can inherit it when a spouse or minor child survives. Homestead questions are a frequent reason families hire probate counsel.</p>
<h2 id="avoid">Assets that skip probate</h2>
<p>Jointly owned property with survivorship rights, accounts with a payable-on-death beneficiary, life insurance and retirement accounts with named beneficiaries, and assets in a living trust usually pass outside probate.</p>
<p>Handling a loved one's estate? <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=probate">Request a free probate review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="bankruptcy-chapter-7-vs-13", cat="Money disputes", issue="bankruptcy", read=8,
title="Chapter 7 vs. Chapter 13 Bankruptcy in South Florida",
desc="How federal bankruptcy works at the C. Clyde Atkins U.S. Courthouse: the means test, credit counseling, the automatic stay, the 341 meeting and what you can keep.",
body="""
<p>Bankruptcy is a federal process. In Miami, cases are filed in the <strong>U.S. Bankruptcy Court for the Southern District of Florida</strong>, headquartered at the C. Clyde Atkins U.S. Courthouse, <strong>301 N. Miami Avenue (33128)</strong>.</p>
<h2 id="compare">The two consumer chapters</h2>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Chapter 7</th><th>Chapter 13</th></tr>
<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Liquidation: a trustee sells non-exempt assets; most unsecured debts are discharged</td><td>Repayment plan over 3–5 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical timeline</td><td>About 4–6 months</td><td>3–5 years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eligibility</td><td>Must pass the "means test" (income compared with state median)</td><td>Regular income; debts under statutory limits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Lower income, few non-exempt assets</td><td>Saving a home from foreclosure, catching up arrears, protecting non-exempt assets</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="before">Before you file</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Credit counseling</strong> from an approved agency is required within 180 days before filing.</li>
<li>Gather two to four years of tax returns, recent pay stubs, bank statements and a full list of debts.</li>
<li>Avoid running up new debt or repaying relatives right before filing — trustees can unwind preferential payments.</li></ul>
<h2 id="stay">The automatic stay</h2>
<p>Filing immediately stops most collection actions — calls, lawsuits, wage garnishments and foreclosure sales — while the case is pending.</p>
<h2 id="341">The 341 meeting of creditors</h2>
<p>About a month after filing, you answer questions under oath from the trustee. Creditors rarely attend. Bring photo ID and proof of your Social Security number.</p>
<h2 id="keep">What you can keep</h2>
<p>Florida has generous exemptions — notably the homestead exemption for a primary residence (subject to acreage and federal residency rules) — plus exemptions for a vehicle up to a set value, retirement accounts and certain wages. Exemptions are where a good bankruptcy attorney earns their fee.</p>
<h2 id="not">Debts usually not discharged</h2>
<p>Most student loans, recent taxes, child support and alimony, and debts from fraud generally survive bankruptcy.</p>
<p>Wondering if you qualify? <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=bankruptcy">Get a free bankruptcy review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="car-accident-miami", cat="Injury", issue="injury", read=9,
title="After a Car Accident in Miami: Florida's No-Fault Rules, the 14-Day Window and Deadlines",
desc="What to do after a Miami crash: Florida PIP, the 14-day medical treatment rule, modified comparative negligence, the two-year deadline and how injury claims are valued.",
body="""
<p>Miami-Dade has some of the busiest roads in Florida. If you're hurt in a crash, a few early decisions — especially in the first two weeks — have an outsized effect on what you can recover.</p>
<h2 id="scene">At the scene</h2>
<ol><li>Check for injuries and call 911. Florida requires crashes involving injury, death or significant property damage to be reported.</li>
<li>Photograph vehicles, plates, the scene, traffic signals and your injuries.</li>
<li>Exchange driver, insurance and registration details; get witness names and numbers.</li>
<li>Don't admit fault or speculate — stick to facts.</li></ol>
<h2 id="pip">Florida no-fault insurance (PIP)</h2>
<p>Florida drivers must carry <strong>$10,000 in Personal Injury Protection</strong>. Your own PIP pays a share of medical bills and lost wages regardless of who caused the crash.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>The 14-day rule:</strong> to use PIP benefits, you generally must receive initial medical treatment within <strong>14 days</strong> of the accident. Get checked even if you feel fine — some injuries show up later.</div>
<h2 id="beyond">Stepping outside no-fault</h2>
<p>To sue the at-fault driver for pain and suffering, Florida law requires a serious injury threshold — such as significant and permanent loss of a bodily function, permanent injury, significant scarring or disfigurement. Economic losses beyond PIP can also be pursued.</p>
<h2 id="fault">Comparative negligence changed in 2023</h2>
<p>Since March 2023, Florida uses <strong>modified comparative negligence</strong>: if you're found more than 50% at fault, you generally recover nothing. At 50% or less, your recovery is reduced by your percentage of fault. Medical-negligence cases are treated differently.</p>
<h2 id="deadline">The deadline</h2>
<p>For negligence claims arising after March 24, 2023, the statute of limitations in Florida is generally <strong>two years</strong> (it was four years before). Missing it usually ends your claim permanently.</p>
<h2 id="value">How claims are valued</h2>
<p>Insurers consider medical expenses, future treatment, lost income, property damage and non-economic damages (pain, suffering, loss of enjoyment of life). A rough teaching method is the "multiplier" approach — try our <a href="../tools.html#injury">claim illustrator</a> — but real values depend on liability, policy limits and evidence.</p>
<h2 id="lawyer">Do you need an injury lawyer?</h2>
<p>Most injury attorneys work on <strong>contingency</strong> — no fee unless they recover money, typically a percentage set by Florida Bar rules. For anything beyond minor soft-tissue claims, a consultation costs nothing and can protect against early lowball offers.</p>
<p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="../free-case-review.html?issue=injury">Get a free accident case review →</a></p>
"""),

dict(slug="recording-a-deed-miami-dade", cat="Property", issue="real-estate", read=6,
title="How to Record a Deed in Miami-Dade County",
desc="Recording deeds and official records at Courthouse East (22 NW 1st St.): Florida formatting rules, witnesses, notarization, documentary stamp tax and e-recording.",
body="""
<p>Recording a deed puts the world on notice of who owns a property. In Miami-Dade, the <strong>County Recorder's Office</strong> is on the first floor of <strong>Courthouse East, 22 NW 1st Street (33128)</strong>, and many documents can also be e-recorded through approved vendors.</p>
<h2 id="format">Florida formatting requirements</h2>
<ul><li>A blank space of 3 inches by 3 inches at the top right of the first page, and 1 inch by 3 inches on following pages, for the clerk's recording information.</li>
<li>Name and address of the person who prepared the document.</li>
<li>Legible names of each person who signed, witnessed or notarized it.</li>
<li>The grantee's (new owner's) mailing address.</li></ul>
<h2 id="witness">Witnesses and notary</h2>
<p>Florida deeds conveying real property must be signed in the presence of <strong>two subscribing witnesses</strong> and notarized. Witness names and addresses should be printed beneath their signatures. Remote online notarization is legal in Florida when done through a registered provider.</p>
<h2 id="tax">Documentary stamp tax</h2>
<p>Florida charges documentary stamp tax on deeds based on the consideration paid. <strong>Miami-Dade uses a different rate structure from the rest of the state</strong>, including a surtax on many transfers that are not single-family residences. Check the current rates with the Clerk or the Florida Department of Revenue before recording.</p>
<h2 id="after">After recording</h2>
<p>Recorded documents become searchable public records. Homeowners should then file for the homestead exemption with the Property Appraiser by the annual deadline if the home is their primary residence.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Watch out for title fraud.</strong> Miami-Dade offers free property-fraud alert services that notify owners when documents are recorded in their name — worth signing up for.</div>
<p>Buying, selling or transferring to family? <a href="../free-case-review.html?issue=real-estate">Get a real-estate attorney review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="how-to-choose-a-lawyer", cat="Getting help", issue="other", read=6,
title="How to Choose a Lawyer in Miami: 12 Questions to Ask at Your First Consultation",
desc="Verify a Florida attorney, understand hourly vs. flat vs. contingency fees, and use these 12 consultation questions to hire the right lawyer for your case.",
body="""
<p>Miami has thousands of practicing attorneys. The right one for your matter combines relevant experience, clear communication and a fee arrangement you understand.</p>
<h2 id="verify">Verify first</h2>
<p>Every lawyer practicing Florida law must be a member in good standing of <strong>The Florida Bar</strong>. Its public member search shows admission date, contact information and any public discipline. Federal practice also requires admission to the relevant federal court.</p>
<h2 id="fees">Understand the fee models</h2>
<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Common in</th><th>What to ask</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hourly + retainer</td><td>Family, business litigation</td><td>Rates for partner/associate/paralegal; billing increments; replenishment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat fee</td><td>Simple wills, uncontested divorce, Chapter 7, traffic</td><td>Exactly what's included and what triggers extra fees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contingency</td><td>Personal injury, some employment cases</td><td>Percentage at each stage; who pays costs if you lose</td></tr></table>
<p>Use our <a href="../tools.html#cost">legal cost estimator</a> to model hourly scenarios.</p>
<h2 id="questions">12 questions to ask</h2>
<ol><li>How many cases like mine have you handled in the last two years?</li>
<li>Who will actually work on my file day to day?</li>
<li>What's your honest assessment of my strengths and weaknesses?</li>
<li>What outcomes are realistic — best, likely and worst case?</li>
<li>What's the expected timeline?</li>
<li>How do you bill, and can I get the agreement in writing?</li>
<li>What costs (filing, experts, depositions) should I expect beyond your fee?</li>
<li>How and how quickly do you return calls and emails?</li>
<li>Do you see a path to settle or resolve this without trial?</li>
<li>What do you need from me right now?</li>
<li>Are there deadlines I'm at risk of missing?</li>
<li>If we disagree on strategy, how do we handle it?</li></ol>
<h2 id="flags">Red flags</h2>
<ul><li>Guarantees of a specific result.</li><li>Pressure to sign on the spot.</li><li>Vague fee terms or refusal to put them in writing.</li></ul>
<p>Skip the search: describe your situation once and we'll route it to a suitable attorney. <a href="../free-case-review.html">Start a free case review</a>.</p>
"""),

dict(slug="criminal-case-first-steps", cat="Criminal", issue="criminal", read=7,
title="Arrested or Charged in Miami-Dade? The First 72 Hours Explained",
desc="First appearance, bond, the public defender, arraignment and the most common mistakes people make right after an arrest in Miami-Dade County.",
body="""
<p>An arrest is disorienting for the person charged and their family. Understanding the first steps helps you avoid mistakes that are hard to undo.</p>
<h2 id="first">First appearance</h2>
<p>Florida requires that a person who is arrested and held be brought before a judge within <strong>24 hours</strong>. At this hearing the judge reviews probable cause, sets release conditions or bond, and advises the person of the right to counsel.</p>
<h2 id="bond">Bond and pretrial release</h2>
<p>Release can be on recognizance, on a cash or surety bond, or with conditions such as pretrial supervision or monitoring. Some offenses follow a standard bond schedule; others require a hearing. Family members can usually post bond through the corrections department or a licensed bail bond agent.</p>
<h2 id="counsel">Right to a lawyer</h2>
<p>If you can't afford a lawyer, the court can appoint the Public Defender. If you hire private counsel, do it early — the first days are when evidence is preserved, witnesses are located and charging decisions can be influenced.</p>
<h2 id="silent">The most common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Talking about the case</strong> — to police, cellmates or on recorded jail phone lines. Jail calls are recorded.</li>
<li><strong>Posting on social media.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Contacting the alleged victim</strong>, especially when a no-contact order is in place.</li>
<li><strong>Missing a court date</strong>, which can lead to a warrant and bond forfeiture.</li></ul>
<h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
<p>After formal charges are filed, there is an arraignment where a plea is entered, followed by discovery, motions, plea negotiations and, if needed, trial. Diversion programs may be available for some first-time or low-level offenses.</p>
<h2 id="federal">Federal charges</h2>
<p>Federal criminal cases in Miami are heard at the Wilkie D. Ferguson, Jr. U.S. Courthouse (400 N. Miami Ave., 33128) and follow different rules, including federal sentencing guidelines. Federal cases demand counsel with federal experience.</p>
<p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="../free-case-review.html?issue=criminal">Request a confidential defense review →</a></p>
"""),
]
